## Solution

Let $f(x,y) = (e^{2x+y},\, e^{x+y})$. Set $u = e^{2x+y}$, $v = e^{x+y}$, so $u,v > 0$.

**Inverting the map.** Taking logarithms:
$$\ln u = 2x + y, \qquad \ln v = x + y.$$
Subtracting gives $x = \ln u - \ln v = \ln(u/v)$, and then $y = \ln v - x = 2\ln v - \ln u = \ln(v^2/u)$. For every $(u,v)\in(0,\infty)^2$ this yields a unique $(x,y)\in\mathbb{R}^2$, so $f$ is a bijection $\mathbb{R}^2 \to (0,\infty)^2$.

**Forward Jacobian.**
$$J_f(x,y) = \begin{pmatrix} 2e^{2x+y} & e^{2x+y} \\ e^{x+y} & e^{x+y} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2u & u \\ v & v \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \det J_f = 2uv - uv = uv > 0.$$

**Inverse Jacobian.**
$$\det J_{f^{-1}}(u,v) = \frac{1}{\det J_f(x,y)} = \frac{1}{uv}.$$

**Density of the pushforward.** Since $f$ is a $C^1$-diffeomorphism from $\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $(0,\infty)^2$, the change-of-variables formula gives, for any Borel set $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$:
$$f[\lambda_2](A) = \lambda_2(f^{-1}(A)) = \int_{A\cap(0,\infty)^2} \left|\det J_{f^{-1}}(u,v)\right|\,du\,dv = \int_{A\cap(0,\infty)^2} \frac{1}{uv}\,du\,dv.$$

Therefore the Radon–Nikodym density of $f[\lambda_2]$ with respect to $\lambda_2$ is:

$$\boxed{\frac{df[\lambda_2]}{d\lambda_2}(u,v) = \begin{cases} \dfrac{1}{uv}, & (u,v)\in(0,\infty)^2,\\[6pt] 0, & \text{otherwise.} \end{cases}}$$

